# Nuclear 01 — The radon decay chain (Bateman equations)
#
# (Krane, "Introductory Nuclear Physics", §6.4, radioactive decay chains.)  A filter
# that sampled radon-laden air holds, at t = 0, 1000 Bq of freshly collected
# Po-218 and none of its daughters.  The chain is
#
#   Po-218 --3.098 min--> Pb-214 --26.8 min--> Bi-214 --19.9 min--> Po-214 --164.3 μs--> Pb-210
#
# (Pb-210, t½ = 22.2 yr, counts as stable here.)
#  (a) Find the activity of every member at t = 10, 30 and 60 min.
#  (b) When is the Bi-214 activity largest, and how large is it?
#  (c) Show that Po-214 is in secular equilibrium with Bi-214 (equal activity).

λ1 = ln(2) / (3.098 min)
λ2 = ln(2) / (26.8 min)
λ3 = ln(2) / (19.9 min)
λ4 = ln(2) / (164.3 μs)
N0 = 1000 Bq / λ1

solve N1' = -λ1 N1,
      N2' = λ1 N1 - λ2 N2,
      N3' = λ2 N2 - λ3 N3,
      N4' = λ3 N3 - λ4 N4
  with N1(0) = N0, N2(0) = 0, N3(0) = 0, N4(0) = 0
  for t from 0 min to 120 min

for T in [10 min, 30 min, 60 min]
    print "t =", T, " A(Po-218) =", λ1 N1(T) in Bq to 8 digits, " A(Pb-214) =", λ2 N2(T) in Bq to 8 digits,
          " A(Bi-214) =", λ3 N3(T) in Bq to 8 digits, " A(Po-214) =", λ4 N4(T) in Bq to 8 digits

# The peak is where dN3/dt = 0.  (`solve N3'(t_max) = 0 for t_max ...` is read
# as a differential equation, so the derivative goes through a helper.)
slope3(tt) = N3'(tt)
solve slope3(t_max) = 0 for t_max from 1 min to 120 min
print "Bi-214 activity peaks at", t_max in min to 8 digits, "with", λ3 N3(t_max) in Bq to 8 digits
print "A(Po-214) / A(Bi-214) at 60 min =", λ4 N4(60 min) / (λ3 N3(60 min)) to 8 digits
